86045417 true] [17592186431703 1078 #edb/ref 17592186431685 13194139534663 17592186045417 true] [17592186431703 1079 #uuid "ba4fda4c-a5fe-531d-a4cc-08d2ea5d8a53" 13194139534663 17592186045417 true] [17592186431704 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431681 13194139534663 17592186045417 true] [17592186431704 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431688 13194139534663 17592186045417 true] [17592186431704 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431689 13194139534663 17592186045417 true] [17592186431704 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431695 13194139534663 17592186045417 true] [17592186431704 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431698 13194139534663 17592186045417 true] [17592186431704 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534663 17592186045417 true] [17592186431704 1077 "selection" 13194139534663 17592186045417 true] [17592186431704 1078 #edb/ref 17592186431689 13194139534663 17592186045417 true] [17592186431704 1079 #uuid "bcb53027-c7e0-55e6-85b6-4d8da4b5b3e6" 13194139534663 17592186045417 true] [17592186431705 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431701 13194139534663 17592186045417 true] [17592186431705 1069 "q-162163" 13194139534663 17592186045417 true] [17592186431705 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534663 17592186045417 true] [17592186431705 1071 "The equation below describes all the points on the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(-1,2,-3)$ and $Q(-2,0,4).$\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ a \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ b\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [c,1]\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" 13194139534663 17592186045417 true] [17592186431705 1072 #uuid "09c49afc-6085-50e6-863a-8dee0d62b478" 13194139534663 17592186045417 true] [17592186431705 1074 "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\nr=p+tv\\,⟹\\,r=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand the vector equation of the  line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=2+7+0=9.\n$$" 13194139534663 17592186045417 true] [17592186431706 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431702 13194139534663 17592186045417 true] [17592186431706 1069 "q-126965" 13194139534663 17592186045417 true] [17592186431706 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534663 17592186045417 true] [17592186431706 1071 "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(3,3,1)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨-4,-2,4⟩.$ Find all the possible points $A$ on that line such that $AP=3.$" 13194139534663 17592186045417 true] [17592186431706 1072 #uuid "194bde74-7427-5189-95d5-f403b6acd3c5" 13194139534663 17592186045417 true] [17592186431706 1074 "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =⟨3,3,1⟩+t⟨-4,-2,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSince $A$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OA} & =⟨3,3,1⟩+t⟨-4,-2,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PA} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨3,3,1⟩+t⟨-4,-2,4⟩)-⟨3,3,1⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,⟨-4,-2,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4t,\\,-2t,\\,4t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PA=3,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AP & =3 \\\\ |\\overset{\\to }{PA}| & =3 \\\\ |⟨-4t,\\,-2t,\\,4t⟩| & =3 \\\\ \\sqrt{(-4t)^{2}+(-2t)^{2}+(4t)^{2}} & =3 \\\\ \\sqrt{16{t}^{2}+4{t}^{2}+16{t}^{2}} & =3 \\\\ \\sqrt{36{t}^{2}} & =3 \\\\ \\pm 6t & =3 \\\\ t & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm \\frac{1}{2}$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{1}}=⟨3,3,1⟩+\\frac{1}{2}⟨-4,-2,4⟩=⟨1,2,3⟩, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{2}}=⟨3,3,1⟩-\\frac{1}{2}⟨-4,-2,4⟩=⟨5,4,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are ${A}_{1}(1,2,3)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,4,-1).$" 13194139534663 17592186045417 true] [17592186431707 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431700 13194139534663 17592186045417 true] [17592186431707 1069 "q-126432" 13194139534663 17592186045417 true] [17592186431707 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534663 17592186045417 true] [17592186431707 1071 "Find a vector equation of the line that passes through the points $A(2,-3,5)$ and $B(3,1,-2).$" 13194139534663 17592186045417 true] [17592186431707 1072 #uuid "5536a355-6822-5a47-a0a6-be384dfb79e8" 13194139534663 17592186045417 true] [17592186431707 1074 "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $A$ and parallel to $v$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=a+tv,\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $a$ is the position vector of $A.$ \n\nWe can find a direction vector by computing the displacement vector between the given points $A(2,-3,5)$ and $B(3,1,-2)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{AB} \\\\  & =⟨3,1,-2⟩-⟨2,-3,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,4,-7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector equation of the line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534663 17592186045417 true] [17592186431708 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431703 13194139534663 17592186045417 true] [17592186431708 1069 "q-69807" 13194139534663 17592186045417 true] [17592186431708 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534663 17592186045417 true] [17592186431708 1071 "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(3,2,1)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨1,\\,3,\\,0⟩.$ Find all the possible points $Y$ on that line such that $YP=\\sqrt{160}.$" 13194139534663 17592186045417 true] [17592186431708 1072 #uuid "c91c661d-e879-5f90-a5fb-ceb93bf19954" 13194139534663 17592186045417 true] [17592186431708 1074 "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =⟨3,\\,2,\\,1⟩+t⟨1,\\,3,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSince $Y$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OY} & =⟨3,\\,2,\\,1⟩+t⟨1,\\,3,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PY} & =\\overset{\\to }{OY}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨3,\\,2,\\,1⟩+t⟨1,\\,3,\\,0⟩)-⟨3,\\,2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =t⟨1,\\,3,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t,\\,3t,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PY=\\sqrt{160},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}YP & =\\sqrt{160} \\\\ |\\overset{\\to }{PY}| & =\\sqrt{160} \\\\ |⟨t,\\,3t,\\,0⟩| & =\\sqrt{160} \\\\ \\sqrt{{t}^{2}+(3t)^{2}+{0}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{160} \\\\ \\sqrt{10{t}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{160} \\\\ \\pm \\sqrt{10}t & =4\\sqrt{10} \\\\ t & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 4$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{O{Y}_{1}}=⟨3,\\,2,\\,1⟩+4⟨1,\\,3,\\,0⟩=⟨7,14,1⟩, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{O{Y}_{2}}=⟨3,\\,2,\\,1⟩-4⟨1,\\,3,\\,0⟩=⟨-1,-10,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are ${Y}_{1}(7,14,1)$ and ${Y}_{2}(-1,-10,1).$" 13194139534663 17592186045417 true] [17592186431709 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431704 13194139534663 17592186045417 true] [17592186431709 1069 "q-35972" 13194139534663 17592186045417 true] [17592186431709 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534663 17592186045417 true] [17592186431709 1071 "Find a vector equation of the line that passes through the points $M(-1,-2,-3)$ and $N(2,3,4).$" 13194139534663 17592186045417 true] [17592186431709 1072 #uuid "cf9c9d04-5ab5-558b-bf38-eae2eb3b3fea" 13194139534663 17592186045417 true] [17592186431709 1074 "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $M$